w they needed but it's cemented  itself as one of the directors most  important in well-regarded films  thanks to Burton's incredibly earnest  approach in his biography of wood who is  regarded as one of the worst directors  in history he conversely managed to  cement a legacy for himself as a great  one  Burton's comedy horror classic feel  juice has been a cult favorite since its  release back in 1988 and with good  reason as Burton's breakout film it  tells the terrifyingly yet hilarious  story of a young undead couple who end  up haunting their former home and trying  their best torment its new human  inhabitants unfortunately they're also  accompanied by a particularly irritating  poltergeist named beetlejuice but  critics fell in love with the film long  before it became a cult classic those  critics lavished praise on the movie  which also marks the first-ever  collaboration between Keaton and Burton  led by Keaton's delightfully bombastic  yet carefully crafted performances  Beetlejuice in Cho dad  the film allowed Burton's spookier  sensibilities to play off freely  resulting in a movie that still holds up  as a classic today in the aftermath of  its theatrical debut it ended up winning  an Academy Award for Best makeup and  even spawned a few spin-offs including a  handful of video games and even a  Broadway musical when people think of  Tim Burton it's likely their minds will  go right to Edward Scissorhands and  honestly it's pretty easy to see why  released in 1990 this signature Burton  film tells the incredible and tragic  tale of a manufactured human named  Edward who remains unfinished with crude  scissors serving as his hands when he  wanders out of his home he finds  sanctuary with the suburban Boggs family  with the help of this new family Edward  does his best to fit in but soon  discovers that society has never been  kind outcasts and weirdos thanks to its  fantastical imagery a superb performance  by Depp and a wide array of  larger-than-life 